### NEW commit messages (c46c41fc..HEAD)
=== 2ba2f05a test(weather): pin Met Office's first real failure, correct comments
Met Office logs a failed fetch above the gate with failure_log_level. The
shared tests only looked at the replay, so a hard-coded DEBUG at either of the
two Met Office sites would have hidden every outage of that service and still
passed. A twin of the Open-Meteo accessor test now pins that the first real
failure of get_data and get_forecast_data is one ERROR record and that the
replay inside the pause logs nothing above DEBUG.

An exception the gate cannot copy (its constructor refuses, or __copy__ returns
the instance itself) is now kept and raised again unmarked. The second try that
rebuilt it without running its constructor is gone: an instance whose class
never initialised it is a worse thing to hand out than one extra ERROR line.
Before, a __copy__ that returned the instance itself got the real exception
marked as a replay. One test covers both ways of not being copyable.

Comments and test notes that said more than is true or pointed at one install:
- the copy kept for a pause has no traceback of its own, but exceptions chained
  inside it (requests wraps urllib3's MaxRetryError in args[0]) keep theirs,
  bounded to one chain per gate and shared with the first caller's real
  exception on purpose;
- cache_seconds changes when the user lengthens the update interval, not by
  itself in an outage;
- measurements are described as taken on one live install, not "the test
  install" or "the production install";
- the NOT-TO-DO on the asyncio lock keeps its rule in one sentence: the hourly
  accessors on the event loop only read stored documents and never reach a gate;
- the Pirate Weather retry comment states why the response noted before a later
  network exception never carries a Retry-After.

Test housekeeping: the module docstring covers the whole gate, a helper
parameter no longer shadows the requests import, and the Pirate Weather case
table asserts its size so a renamed id cannot empty four tests. An HTTP-date
Retry-After is now checked against a clock whose zoneless now() reads two hours
ahead; freezegun's tz_offset shifts now(tz) as well, so it cannot tell the two
clocks apart.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 2caf6f1e fix(weather): Pirate Weather pauses between retries and stops on 429
Both Pirate Weather fetch bodies asked up to three times back to back,
whatever the answer. A short gateway fault had no time to clear between
the requests, and a 429, which at Pirate Weather means the monthly quota
is used up, cannot turn into a 200 a second later: the retries were only
more requests on a key that is already refused.

The two copies of the retry loop are now one method, _get_with_retries.
It waits 1 s before the second attempt and 2 s before the third, and
nothing after the last, so a failing call takes at most 3 s longer
against a 60 s timeout per attempt. A 429, or any response that names a
time with Retry-After, ends the attempts at once; the caller's non-200
branch then returns None and the gate's pause (which honours the header)
takes over. The method does not wait out Retry-After itself: that can be
up to a day, and it would hold an executor thread under the gate's lock.

Everything else is unchanged: three attempts, a retry only on a status
other than 200, network exceptions are not retried, the error log and the
parsing after the loop. Each response is still handed to the gate. Since a
response with Retry-After ends the loop, the response noted before a later
network exception never carries one. The dead "i = i + 1" goes.

The tests replace the client module's own name for time, not time.sleep
itself, so no failing run waits for real and the tests that sleep in the
test file keep the real sleep.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== efcdf823 fix(weather): the pause after a failure honours Retry-After
A service (or a gateway or proxy in front of it) that answers 429 or 503
may say when it wants to be asked again. None of the four services
documents the header, so this is a safeguard; but a request before that
moment is likely to be refused again.

The pause after a failed fetch is now the longer of the existing backoff
and the Retry-After of the failing response, at most one day. "Honour"
means not earlier, so the header is a lower bound: a short value never
shortens the backoff, and the cap keeps an absurd or misread value from
shutting a gate for good. The header is read as seconds or as an HTTP
date (RFC 9110 section 10.2.3); anything missing, unreadable, not a
string or already due counts as absent.

Open-Meteo and Met Office raise an HTTPError that carries the response.
OWM and Pirate Weather do not let the response out on a bad status (an
OSError, a None), so they hand it to their gate with note_response right
after each request.

Three carried fixes in the same code, each with its own test:

- Pin that a fresh entry is served before the pause is looked at.
  Lengthening the update interval during an outage raises cache_seconds
  (it changes only when the user edits the update settings), which
  brings an old timestamp back into the window; the entry is fresh
  again and must not be replaced by the replay of the failure. The
  behaviour was already right, but a run that checked the pause first
  passed every test.
- Keep the outcome of a failed run as a copy, made when the failure is
  recorded; the copy has no traceback of its own. The original exception
  held the frames of the failed fetch, and with them a response body or
  a parsed document, until the next fetch. Exceptions chained inside the
  copy keep their tracebacks (requests wraps urllib3's MaxRetryError in
  args[0]): bounded to one chain per gate, and deliberate, since the
  inner exception is shared with the real one the first caller holds.
  Each replay still raises its own fresh copy of it.
- Never mark the original exception as a replay. For a class that cannot
  be copied the gate marked the instance itself; a waiter could then
  mark the first caller's real exception before its accessor logged it,
  and a real failure came out as a DEBUG line. The fallback is to raise
  the stored instance itself, unmarked, so the worst case is one more
  ERROR line.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== bfe1a9ea fix(weather): a replayed failure is logged at DEBUG, not as a fresh error
An ERROR line of a weather client should mean a request was made and
failed. Open-Meteo's and Met Office's public accessors catch a fetch
failure above the gate and logged it at ERROR. Once the gate replays a
failure during its pause, that repeats the same ERROR line for every
caller although no request left the machine, so an outage filled the log
with errors that were not new.

fetch_gate.failure_log_level() returns DEBUG for an exception the gate
replayed and ERROR for anything else. The five accessor log sites
(Open-Meteo get_data, get_forecast_data and get_hourly_data; Met Office
get_data and get_forecast_data) log through it. The message texts and
return values are unchanged, and the first real failure, or a failure to
parse a good response, still logs at ERROR.

OWM and Pirate Weather need no change: their gate wraps the whole
method, so the logging code does not run during the pause.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 6a1ea83a fix(weather): a failed fetch pauses that document instead of being retried at once
After a fetch that left no new timestamp (an exception, or a None after a
non-200 status or an incomplete document), the gate of that fetch point
stays shut: the next callers get that failure's outcome back -- the same
return value, or an exception of the same type and message -- without a
request. The first failure pauses for 60 s; every further failure in a row
doubles the pause, capped at the cache lifetime, and a good fetch resets
it. override_cache still asks every time.

Why: callers arrive every few seconds, and a failed fetch is not stored,
so every one of them (and every caller that had waited behind the failed
run) went to the network again, three times over for Pirate Weather. With
the cap the failure path never asks more often than normal operation does,
which matters for the quota-limited services (Met Office's free tier allows
360 calls a day across its two documents).

The pause is worked out at every check from the owner's current
cache_seconds, so a changed update interval takes effect at once, and it
is measured on the monotonic clock because it is a duration; the freshness
check keeps datetime.now() so the success path does not change. A replayed
exception is a copy marked with is_replay(), so callers that log can tell
it from a fresh failure; one that cannot be copied is raised itself,
unmarked, so it is logged like a fresh failure: the worst case is one more
ERROR line, never a real failure silenced.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 436d66af fix(weather): concurrent callers wait for one fetch instead of each fetching
The weather clients are called from several executor threads at once: every
sensor refresh, skip-condition check and outlook asks for the same document.
Each caller checked the cache, found it empty and went to the network itself,
so a cold cache turned into a burst of identical requests (measured on one
live install with Pirate Weather, 9 zones × 2 schedules: 1 current-conditions
and 18 forecast requests within 0.6 s) on what is often a metered API key.

Put a FetchGate, a thread lock, in front of each of the seven fetch points
(OWM current and forecast, Pirate Weather current and forecast, the Open-Meteo
document, the Met Office hourly and three-hourly documents). The body of each
fetch point is unchanged and moved into a private method; the public name now
runs it through the gate. Its own freshness check therefore runs inside the
lock, so a caller that waited finds the entry the first caller stored and is
served from it.

A thread lock rather than an asyncio one because every path that reaches a
gate runs in an executor thread; the hourly accessors that run on the event
loop only read stored documents and never reach a gate. It has to cover the
whole body rather than the request alone, since the parse and the timestamp
come after the request.

No change on the success path: the same values, timestamps and log lines. One
gate per fetch point, so the current-conditions and forecast documents still
do not wait for each other.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

=== 9a87d9b1 refactor(weather): one freshness check shared by the four weather clients
The "is this cached result still fresh" rule existed three times as a
byte-identical _is_fresh method (OWM, Pirate Weather, Met Office) and once
inline in Open-Meteo's _fetch, next to four copies of the one-minute floor
constant. Met Office also worked the cache lifetime out a second time in
_forecast_document.

Move the rule into weathermodules/fetch_gate.py (MIN_CACHE_SECONDS,
cache_lifetime, is_fresh) and have all four clients call it through the
module, so a later change to how the clients fetch has one place to land and
one patch target in tests.

No behaviour change: the same result is served for the same time, the floor is
still 60 seconds, and nothing on the success path differs. The only
difference is that a cached Open-Meteo document with no timestamp is fetched
again instead of raising, a state the client never produces itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>


